    <!-- Unit Overview -->
    <section class="welcome-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-compass section-icon"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
        <p>The transition from single-item variables to collections. This unit explores how data is organised to improve efficiency in both time (speed of access) and space (memory usage). Key concepts include Contiguous vs Non-contiguous memory, LIFO/FIFO protocols, and Direct vs Sequential access.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Syllabus Links -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark section-icon"></i> What You'll Learn</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>B2.2.1</strong> — Comparing static (fixed-size, contiguous) vs dynamic (resizable, non-contiguous) data structures.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B2.2.2</strong> — Implementation and traversal of 1D and 2D lists (arrays).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B2.2.3</strong> — The Stack ADT: LIFO principle, operations (push, pop, peek, isEmpty).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B2.2.4</strong> — The Queue ADT: FIFO principle, operations (enqueue, dequeue, front, isEmpty).
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

