    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Direct Access:</strong> Students often try to "directly access" an element in a dynamic list using an index without realising that under the hood, some structures require sequential traversal.</li>
            <li><strong>Static Size:</strong> Believing a static array can be resized if it gets "full."</li>
            <li><strong>Stack/Queue Logic:</strong> Mixing up the end where data is added (e.g., trying to pop from the bottom of a stack).</li>
            <li><strong>2D Indices:</strong> Confusing [row][column] order (remember: "RC Cola" or "Rows then Columns").</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Classification as Knowledge:</strong> How does the way we "structure" data change what we can know about it? (e.g., searching a sorted list vs an unsorted one).</li>
                <li><strong>Efficiency vs Truth:</strong> Is an "optimal" algorithm more valuable than one that is easier for humans to understand?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

